This code calculates the number of unique paths with obstacles from the top-left to the bottom-right corner of a grid, where 1 represents an obstacle (cannot step on) and 0 represents an open cell. It extends the standard uniquePaths solution using a 2D DP table with obstacle handling. If the starting cell is an obstacle, it returns 0. The first row and first column are initialized with the condition that each cell's number of ways equals the previous cell's ways only if the current cell is not blocked (obstacles break the path continuity, turning ways to 0). For all other cells, if obstacleGrid[i][j] == 1, dp[i][j] = 0; otherwise, dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1]. Finally, the value at dp[m-1][n-1] is returned.

Time Complexity: O(m × n) — the nested loops iterate over all cells once.
Space Complexity: O(m × n) — for the DP table (though this can be optimized to O(n), the given implementation uses 2D).

class Solution {
    public int uniquePathsWithObstacles(int[][] obstacleGrid) {
        int m = obstacleGrid.length;
        int n = obstacleGrid[0].length;
        int[][] dp = new int[m][n];
        if(obstacleGrid[0][0] == 1) {
            return 0;
        }
        dp[0][0] = 1;
        for(int i = 1; i<n; i++){
            if(obstacleGrid[0][i] == 0) {
                dp[0][i] = dp[0][i-1];
            }
        }
        for(int i = 1; i<m; i++){
            if(obstacleGrid[i][0] == 0) {
                dp[i][0] = dp[i-1][0];
            }
        }
        for(int i = 1; i<m; i++){
            for(int j = 1; j<n; j++) {
                if(obstacleGrid[i][j] == 1) {
                    dp[i][j] = 0;
                }
                else {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1];
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m-1][n-1];
    }
}
